Let \(M\) be passing mathematics and \(P\) be passing physics. We have \(P(M)=0.60\) and \(P(M\cap P)=0.30\). Thus \[
\begin{aligned}
P(P\mid M)&=\frac{P(M\cap P)}{P(M)} \\
&=\frac{0.30}{0.60} \\
&=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\].

Since \(A\) and \(B\) are independent, \[
\begin{aligned}
P(A\cap B)&=P(A)P(B) \\
&=0.5\cdot0.4 \\
&=0.2
\end{aligned}
\]. Then \[
\begin{aligned}
P(A\cup B)&=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cap B) \\
&=0.5+0.4-0.2 \\
&=0.7
\end{aligned}
\].
